WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
DEREK DOWNEY: Hi, I'm Derek
Downey, a Developer Advocate

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.019
for Google Cloud.

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:07.140
I am excited to share some great
news if you are using Cloud

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.750
Spanner and have
downstream systems that

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.530
need to be aware of data
changes in near real time.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.480
Getting data changes from
one system into another,

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.930
especially in real time,
can be challenging.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.880
To address this,
Cloud Spanner just

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.500
released Change
Streams to make it

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.140
easy to set up pipelines
to process changes made

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.010
to your Spanner data.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:30.300
In this video, I will explain
what change streams are

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.640
and what is required
to set them up.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.000
Then I will show you how easy
it is to set up a change stream

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.010
and a pipeline to
process changes

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.880
made to your Spanner data into
a downstream BigQuery analytics

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.330
environment.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:46.290
Finally, I'll go over some
typical use cases for change

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:50.220
streams and how Spanner makes it
easy to enable those use cases.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.184
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.130
So, what are change streams?

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:01.420
If you have ever heard of change
data capture, or CDC, that's

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.340
a good starting point
for understanding change

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.560
streams in Cloud Spanner.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.600
Change streams keep
track of changes

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.680
made to data through DML or
Spanner mutations that can then

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.848
be processed by pipelines to
publish in a downstream system.

00:01:15.848 --> 00:01:17.390
There are many
reasons you might want

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.100
to get data changes from
one system into another.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.040
You might have a data
warehouse and you

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.280
need real time information to
improve business decisions.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.920
You might want to trigger
downstream application

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.770
workflows, for instance,
restocking an item

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.890
if the inventory level
drops beneath a threshold.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:36.650
You might have a need to keep
an audit trail of changes

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.320
in a separate system
for compliance purposes,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.960
or perhaps you want to
keep track of changes

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.030
for disaster recovery.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:47.180
Whatever the reason, the process
to set up a change stream

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.640
in Spanner is straightforward.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.900
First, create the change
stream in your database.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.780
A change stream is another
type of object in Spanner,

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.400
like a table or a
secondary index.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.810
So you can add, modify, or
delete change stream objects

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.900
using DDL.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.640
You can create a
single change stream

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.850
for all tables in
a database, or you

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.300
can create a change stream
for specific tables or even

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.570
specific columns
within those tables.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:19.080
Second, you need a destination
to receive the changes.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.950
This can be BigQuery, Google
Cloud Storage, Google Pub/Sub,

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:28.090
or any other system to
receive the stream of changes.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.250
And finally, you
set up a pipeline

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:33.490
to process change events from
the change stream and Spanner

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.440
and publish to your
downstream system.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.710
You can easily build
data flow pipelines

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:40.720
to process the
change stream events,

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.740
leveraging Spanner I/O from
the Apache Beam repository,

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:47.770
or you can connect
directly to the Spanner API

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.010
and write your own
processing logic.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:54.080
This might be best to
explain with an example.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.350
I currently have an inventory
service for an e-commerce store

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.450
that is backed by Spanner.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.810
I also have BigQueries set up
to track inventory behavior

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.470
over time.

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.150
Having this
analytics environment

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.280
has enabled me to
make good decisions

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.700
on what items to keep in stock.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.090
Currently, to get data
from Spanner into BigQuery,

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.820
I had to set up and maintain
additional pipelines

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.550
to extract the data in batches
before loading into BigQuery.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:24.740
These batches cause
some delays that

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:28.310
have caused headaches
for my analysts.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:31.760
With change streams, I can move
to almost real time streaming

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.700
of data changes into
BigQuery and simplify

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.540
the logic for processing.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.450
To set up my change stream,
I go into the Cloud Console

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.340
page for my Spanner instance.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.570
Then I add my change
stream via the DDL page.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:49.040
The syntax is fairly
straightforward.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.860
You need a change stream name.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.710
Change stream names are unique
to the Spanner database.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.800
Next, you specify
the entities you want

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.630
to track in this change stream.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:01.870
You can track the entire
database, a set of tables,

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:04.850
or specific columns for tables.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:09.110
Last, you specify the retention
period for change stream data.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:10.970
The records that are
tracked are stored

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:15.070
for this retention period before
automatically cleaning up.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:18.070
By default, the retention
period is 24 hours,

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:20.200
but can range up to 7 days.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.110
And you'll pay only for the
additional storage required

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.780
to keep the change
events longer.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:28.870
Now, I also have my
BigQuery environment set up

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.260
that is used for
inventory analytics.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:37.160
All I have to do is set up the
Dataflow pipeline for BigQuery.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.980
So, I go into Dataflow
in the Cloud Console,

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.580
then create a new pipeline.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.920
I provide the pipeline and name.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.260
The region I select
is "us-central1"

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:50.210
to be near my Spanner instance.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:54.120
And then I select the Cloud
Spanner to BigQuery template.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.200
This template leverages
the Apache Beam Spanner I/O

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.720
connector.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.000
Here I need to provide
some more information.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.120
First is the Spanner instance
name and database name.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.100
This is the location
of the change stream

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.700
object I created earlier.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.670
Next, I need to provide a
metadata instance and database

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:14.060
name.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.130
This is needed for the
Spanner I/O connector

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:19.160
to keep track of the
changes it has processed.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.810
The metadata
instance and database

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.990
can be the same as where the
change stream is located,

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.390
but I'd recommend at
least storing the metadata

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:29.160
in its own database.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.720
Once we've provided
the metadata location,

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.800
then we provide the
change stream name.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.980
And finally, I specify
a BigQuery data

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:39.710
set to store the data changes.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:41.545
There are some
optional parameters,

00:05:41.545 --> 00:05:42.920
but this is enough
to get started

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:44.930
processing the change
streams from when

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.390
the pipeline is created.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:48.200
That's it.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.750
After a few minutes for
the pipeline to get set up,

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.895
I will start receiving
data changes into BigQuery.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:57.590
As part of releasing
change streams,

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.140
the Spanner team has
also released Spanner I/O

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:02.390
to the Apache Beam repository.

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:05.630
Spanner I/O can be used to
process data change events

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.540
from Spanner change streams.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:11.240
There are also three main
Dataflow pipeline templates

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:14.060
available that leverage
Spanner I/O to quickly

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:18.730
get started processing
Spanner change streams.

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:20.310
The first is the
BigQuery template

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:21.990
that I just walked through.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:24.120
This allows you
to set up BigQuery

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:28.360
to receive changes from
Spanner in near real time.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.720
There is also a Google
Cloud Storage template.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.810
One example of sending data
changes to Cloud Storage

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:38.230
would be to keep an audit
archive of detail change events

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:42.160
on important data for
regulatory purposes.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.880
And finally, there is a
template to publish data changes

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.170
to Pub/Sub.

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.180
This is useful to
trigger workflows

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:50.160
in external
applications that are

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.560
listening on a downstream
Pub/Sub related

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:53.940
to those changes.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:57.060
If you need to publish data
changes to another destination

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.270
or if you need additional
processing of the data changes,

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:02.430
then you can create
a custom pipeline

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:06.760
that leverages the Apache
Beam Spanner I/O connector.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.700
It is also possible to connect
directly to the change stream

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:13.960
API to create a customized
data pipeline without using

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:16.620
the Spanner I/O connector.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:18.360
It's possible to
have multiple change

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:21.630
streams in the same database
and even multiple change

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:24.570
streams tracking the
same tables or columns.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.210
Here are some things to keep
in mind with multiple change

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.200
streams.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.050
You can have up to 10
change streams per database

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:35.880
and up to 3 change streams can
track the same non-key column.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.490
You should maintain no more
than 5 concurrent readers

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:43.510
for a change stream partition
or performance may degrade.

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:46.060
Change streams is a great
new feature in Spanner

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.250
that enables real
time data pipelines

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:50.110
based on data changes.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:52.660
I have shown how to
set up a basic pipeline

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:55.390
to get Spanner data
changes into BigQuery

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:57.760
using the available
Dataflow template.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.340
There are many ways change
streams can be leveraged,

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:03.100
from triggering downstream
application workflows using

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.950
Pub/Sub, storing data
within Cloud Storage

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:08.920
to enable data recovery,
or for setting up an audit

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:10.870
trail for compliance purposes.

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:13.550
Let us know in the comments how
you're using change streams,

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:15.583
and if there are other
templates you'd like

00:08:15.583 --> 00:08:16.750
us to provide in the future.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:20.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

